# The `build-player` tool (caos SPEC, "Tools"): this directory's worker script
# curried onto the Nim worker image, plus the `help` an agent registers it by.
#
# The docs are a HERE-STRING, so they live here rather than in the script's
# header — and a `.caos-expr` is STRIPPED from the tree its own expression is
# evaluated against, so editing them re-keys this tool's arg tree and NOTHING
# it builds. An agent tuning a bot rewords a `@param` for free.
HELP=<<END
Compile a policy (players/<name>/<name>.nim) inside caos and return its
diagnostics, with the SAME flags players/baseline/Dockerfile ships with — and,
since that Dockerfile builds on this same caos/nim image, the binary this
returns is linked exactly like the shipped one and can be dropped into the
policy image as-is. The Nim deps come from the same cached `deps` job the game build
uses, and the C compilation is ccache-backed, so an unchanged build never runs.
Nothing is handed in from the host: the policy is compiled from source, in a
worker.
@param [player] Which policy under players/ to build — the directory name, whose entry point is players/<name>/<name>.nim. Default: baseline.
@param [defines] Extra defines for the compile, space-separated, e.g. "-d:taunt -d:tuneCarrierFireRange=140". Each must be -d:… or --define:… — this arg is not a general flag escape hatch.
@param [build-salt] Force a rebuild — any fresh value (e.g. $(date --iso=s)) re-keys this build and nothing else.
END
curry --base:@=DEEP-DEPS/nim --worker1:@=worker.sh --help=$HELP
